---
import UseCaseLayout from "../../layouts/UseCaseLayout.astro";
import { saveOrder } from "../../data/demo-answers.ts";
import { explainFlow } from "../../data/page-answers.ts";
---

<UseCaseLayout
  title="Explain a Salesforce Flow in Plain English"
  description="Ask what a Salesforce flow does: when it starts, what it creates or updates, and what happens if it fails. Answers come from the flow's own metadata."
  path="/use-cases/explain-a-salesforce-flow"
  heading="Explain this flow to me"
  lede="Inherited a flow nobody documented? Ask what it does and get when it starts, what it changes, and what goes wrong if it fails, in plain words, without opening Flow Builder."
  crumb="Explain a flow"
  audience="admins and developers"
  short="Ask your AI assistant “what does the Project On Approve flow do?”. sf-intelligence reads the flow’s metadata and explains when it starts, its entry conditions, the records it creates or updates, and risks such as a missing fault path. It reads the flow; it never runs it."
  answers={[explainFlow, saveOrder]}
  answerIntro="One flow on Verdant, the demo org, explained on its own, then shown in the full sequence of what runs when a Project is saved."
  asks={[
    "What does the Project On Approve flow do?",
    "Explain the Case Escalation flow step by step.",
    "What happens if this flow fails?",
    "What runs when an Opportunity is saved?",
  ]}
  checks={[
    "<strong>When it starts</strong>: the object, before or after save, and the entry conditions",
    "<strong>What it changes</strong>: records it creates, updates or deletes, and the fields it sets",
    "<strong>What it calls</strong>: subflows and Apex actions",
    "<strong>Fault handling</strong>: elements with no fault path, and what that rolls back",
    "<strong>Every element in order</strong>, when you ask for the step-by-step walkthrough",
  ]}
  limits={[
    "Which path a specific record takes. That depends on the record’s values",
    "Flows started from code by name, buttons or quick actions you didn’t pull",
    "Changes made since your last refresh",
  ]}
  tools={["explain_flow", "flow_graph", "what_happens_on_save", "what_if_deactivate_flow", "flow_fault_audit"]}
  related={[
    { href: "/blog/what-does-this-salesforce-flow-do", label: "How to read a Salesforce Flow you didn’t build", note: "guide" },
    { href: "/blog/salesforce-order-of-execution", label: "Salesforce order of execution, explained", note: "guide" },
    { href: "/use-cases/impact-analysis", label: "What breaks if I turn this flow off?", note: "use case" },
    { href: "/use-cases/where-is-a-field-used", label: "Where is a field used?", note: "use case" },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: "Does it run the flow or read record data?",
      a: "No. It reads the flow’s metadata from your snapshot. It never runs the flow and never reads records, so it can tell you the conditions a flow checks but not which way a particular record will go.",
    },
    {
      q: "Can it explain a flow step by step?",
      a: "Yes. Ask for a walkthrough and it lists every element in the order it runs, with each element’s own description, the fields on its screens and the inputs to its actions.",
    },
    {
      q: "Why does my flow run twice?",
      a: "Usually because something later in the same save updates the record again, which sets the flow off a second time. Ask “what runs when a Project is saved?” and the answer names any step that writes back to the record. On the demo org nothing does, and the answer says so.",
    },
  ]}
  cta={{ question: explainFlow.question, related: { href: "/blog/what-does-this-salesforce-flow-do", label: "Read the flow-reading guide" } }}
  dateModified="2026-10-10"
>
  <h2>One flow rarely explains the problem</h2>
  <p>If you’re debugging rather than documenting, the reason a field has the wrong value is usually the order things run in, not the flow’s logic. That’s why the second answer above shows the whole save: the before trigger, the validation rule, the save itself, the after trigger, this flow, and the approval process, in the order Salesforce runs them.</p>
  <p>The flow explanation also flags things you’d only find by reading every element. Project On Approve has an element with no fault path, so if it fails, the user’s Project save fails too, not just the flow.</p>
</UseCaseLayout>
